INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$5f6adeee$SATQ$, $SATQ$06e04baa-57bc-4dab-b607-a7f7379fce1f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Biologists have generally believed that the diet of jaguars consists mostly of land-based mammals, but researchers studying a population of jaguars living in the Brazilian Pantanal, a tropical wetland, claim that jaguars can survive on a diet of more fish and aquatic reptiles than mammals.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers&rsquo; claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9351cd7d-e054-4caa-8487-7372740c2d94","content":"<p>Aquatic reptile and fish remains were detected more often than were mammal remains in an analysis of jaguar waste matter found in the area.</p>"},{"id":"55a80115-82c3-4f98-8784-acfe25645c4d","content":"<p>Exceptionally high numbers of yacare caiman, an aquatic reptile, were found living in the area.</p>"},{"id":"4ca56089-57ff-418e-83cd-c95530a1e05b","content":"<p>Aquatic prey like the reptiles and fish in the area provide a source of omega-3 fatty acids that aren&rsquo;t often found in land mammals.</p>"},{"id":"eb75ba10-67fe-4037-be90-60df095f38b3","content":"<p>When jaguars in the area preyed on mammals, they tended to prefer semiaquatic ones like capybaras.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9351cd7d-e054-4caa-8487-7372740c2d94"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would support the researchers&rsquo; claim about the diets of jaguars in the Brazilian Pantanal. The text notes that jaguars generally eat land-based mammals, but researchers claim that jaguars in the Pantanal can survive on a diet that includes more fish and aquatic reptiles than mammals. Finding that the remains of aquatic reptiles and fish appear more often in these jaguars&rsquo; waste than the remains of mammals do would support the researchers&rsquo; claim, since it would suggest that fish and aquatic reptiles are a more significant part of the jaguars&rsquo; diet than mammals are.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because finding that a particular aquatic reptile exists in high numbers in the area would not support the researchers&rsquo; claim about the jaguars&rsquo; diet. The mere presence of many aquatic reptiles nearby does not mean that the jaguars eat those reptiles, let alone survive on more aquatic reptiles and fish than mammals. Choice C is incorrect. Although finding that aquatic reptiles and fish can provide nutrients that land mammals typically do not provide could help explain why some animals eat those reptiles and fish, it would not indicate whether the jaguars in particular eat those reptiles and fish and thus would not support the researchers&rsquo; claim. Choice D is incorrect because finding that when preying on mammals, jaguars prefer semiaquatic mammals, such as capybaras, would be irrelevant to the researchers&rsquo; claim that the jaguars can survive on a diet of more fish and aquatic reptiles than mammals. The type of mammals the jaguars tend to eat does not indicate anything about whether fish and aquatic reptiles are part of the jaguars&rsquo; diet.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5f6adeee","external_id":"06e04baa-57bc-4dab-b607-a7f7379fce1f","vaultid":null,"uId":"4d3177ac-a489-4324-b6de-f68adfc16ee8","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Biologists have generally believed that the diet of jaguars consists mostly of land-based mammals, but researchers studying a population of jaguars living in the Brazilian Pantanal, a tropical wetland, claim that jaguars can survive on a diet of more fish and aquatic reptiles than mammals.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers&rsquo; claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9351cd7d-e054-4caa-8487-7372740c2d94","content":"<p>Aquatic reptile and fish remains were detected more often than were mammal remains in an analysis of jaguar waste matter found in the area.</p>"},{"id":"55a80115-82c3-4f98-8784-acfe25645c4d","content":"<p>Exceptionally high numbers of yacare caiman, an aquatic reptile, were found living in the area.</p>"},{"id":"4ca56089-57ff-418e-83cd-c95530a1e05b","content":"<p>Aquatic prey like the reptiles and fish in the area provide a source of omega-3 fatty acids that aren&rsquo;t often found in land mammals.</p>"},{"id":"eb75ba10-67fe-4037-be90-60df095f38b3","content":"<p>When jaguars in the area preyed on mammals, they tended to prefer semiaquatic ones like capybaras.</p>"}],"keys":["9351cd7d-e054-4caa-8487-7372740c2d94"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would support the researchers&rsquo; claim about the diets of jaguars in the Brazilian Pantanal. The text notes that jaguars generally eat land-based mammals, but researchers claim that jaguars in the Pantanal can survive on a diet that includes more fish and aquatic reptiles than mammals. Finding that the remains of aquatic reptiles and fish appear more often in these jaguars&rsquo; waste than the remains of mammals do would support the researchers&rsquo; claim, since it would suggest that fish and aquatic reptiles are a more significant part of the jaguars&rsquo; diet than mammals are.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because finding that a particular aquatic reptile exists in high numbers in the area would not support the researchers&rsquo; claim about the jaguars&rsquo; diet. The mere presence of many aquatic reptiles nearby does not mean that the jaguars eat those reptiles, let alone survive on more aquatic reptiles and fish than mammals. Choice C is incorrect. Although finding that aquatic reptiles and fish can provide nutrients that land mammals typically do not provide could help explain why some animals eat those reptiles and fish, it would not indicate whether the jaguars in particular eat those reptiles and fish and thus would not support the researchers&rsquo; claim. Choice D is incorrect because finding that when preying on mammals, jaguars prefer semiaquatic mammals, such as capybaras, would be irrelevant to the researchers&rsquo; claim that the jaguars can survive on a diet of more fish and aquatic reptiles than mammals. The type of mammals the jaguars tend to eat does not indicate anything about whether fish and aquatic reptiles are part of the jaguars&rsquo; diet.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323044,"updateDate":1730147323044}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323044, 1730147323044),
    ($SATQ$5fb6ed10$SATQ$, $SATQ$cc73e080-d46d-40ae-bbf6-e9801e577c1a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f478afe0-3c10-4803-b012-18eed503f990$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>The Land of Enchantment</em> is a 1906 travel book by Lilian Whiting. In the book, which describes the experience of traveling through the southwestern United States by train, Whiting reflects on the escape from everyday life that such a journey provides: <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from&nbsp;<em>The Land of Enchantment</em> most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8352eb29-95b2-4b54-b315-6d1230e4e214","content":"<p>&ldquo;The opportunities and advantages already offered and constantly increasing are greater than would at first be considered possible.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"a986fd5e-acdf-4bc6-8c70-351e265adad6","content":"<p>&ldquo;The social and the picturesque charm of the long journey is singularly enhanced by the leisurely stops made for refreshment.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"474e42f7-9689-4a4b-b076-262c31d85325","content":"<p>&ldquo;The real journey begins, of course, at Chicago, and as these trains leave in the evening the traveller fares forth in the seclusion of his berth.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"dd931fa1-d839-4bca-ba94-e958077769b3","content":"<p>&ldquo;One experiences a certain sense of detachment from ordinary day and daylight duties that is exhilarating.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dd931fa1-d839-4bca-ba94-e958077769b3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because. This quotation specifically describes a "sense of detachment from ordinary day," which matches the claim&rsquo;s focus on "escape from everyday life."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While this quotation describes new opportunities associated with Whiting&rsquo;s experience, it doesn&rsquo;t frame these opportunities as "an escape," and it doesn&rsquo;t directly compare Whiting&rsquo;s journey with "everyday life." Choice B is incorrect. While this quotation includes a positive characterization of Whiting&rsquo;s journey, it doesn&rsquo;t specifically address the idea of an "escape from everyday life," which is the focus of the claim. Choice C is incorrect. This quotation focuses on where the journey begins, not on how it feels like an "escape from everyday life."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TXT-M005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$9fdb26f7-d04a-4546-a5bc-223275a5f6d8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5fb6ed10","external_id":"cc73e080-d46d-40ae-bbf6-e9801e577c1a","vaultid":"f478afe0-3c10-4803-b012-18eed503f990","uId":"2ee5018c-be0e-4423-9234-14c2c090ed56","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>The Land of Enchantment</em> is a 1906 travel book by Lilian Whiting. In the book, which describes the experience of traveling through the southwestern United States by train, Whiting reflects on the escape from everyday life that such a journey provides: <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from&nbsp;<em>The Land of Enchantment</em> most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8352eb29-95b2-4b54-b315-6d1230e4e214","content":"<p>&ldquo;The opportunities and advantages already offered and constantly increasing are greater than would at first be considered possible.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"a986fd5e-acdf-4bc6-8c70-351e265adad6","content":"<p>&ldquo;The social and the picturesque charm of the long journey is singularly enhanced by the leisurely stops made for refreshment.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"474e42f7-9689-4a4b-b076-262c31d85325","content":"<p>&ldquo;The real journey begins, of course, at Chicago, and as these trains leave in the evening the traveller fares forth in the seclusion of his berth.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"dd931fa1-d839-4bca-ba94-e958077769b3","content":"<p>&ldquo;One experiences a certain sense of detachment from ordinary day and daylight duties that is exhilarating.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["dd931fa1-d839-4bca-ba94-e958077769b3"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because. This quotation specifically describes a \"sense of detachment from ordinary day,\" which matches the claim&rsquo;s focus on \"escape from everyday life.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While this quotation describes new opportunities associated with Whiting&rsquo;s experience, it doesn&rsquo;t frame these opportunities as \"an escape,\" and it doesn&rsquo;t directly compare Whiting&rsquo;s journey with \"everyday life.\" Choice B is incorrect. While this quotation includes a positive characterization of Whiting&rsquo;s journey, it doesn&rsquo;t specifically address the idea of an \"escape from everyday life,\" which is the focus of the claim. Choice C is incorrect. This quotation focuses on where the journey begins, not on how it feels like an \"escape from everyday life.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TXT-M005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"9fdb26f7-d04a-4546-a5bc-223275a5f6d8","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$5ff1ba73$SATQ$, $SATQ$7eb3995a-1328-4088-bfa1-661911b8e49b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1ea208a1-72cf-4322-b06f-98e062957d5b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Guilds in French Cities in the Late Eighteenth Century</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">City</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Date</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Inhabitants</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Number of guilds</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Inhabitants per guild</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Paris</th><td style="text-align: center;">1766</td><td style="text-align: center;">600,000</td><td style="text-align: center;">133</td><td style="text-align: center;">4,511</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Bordeaux</th><td style="text-align: center;">1762</td><td style="text-align: center;">80,000</td><td style="text-align: center;">49</td><td style="text-align: center;">1,633</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Rouen</th><td style="text-align: center;">1775</td><td style="text-align: center;">74,000</td><td style="text-align: center;">112</td><td style="text-align: center;">661</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Lyon</th><td style="text-align: center;">1789</td><td style="text-align: center;">143,000</td><td style="text-align: center;">72</td><td style="text-align: center;">1,986</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>Guilds&mdash;local associations of artisans and merchants in the same industry&mdash;were widespread in France from the medieval period until the late eighteenth century. But guilds were much more numerous relative to the population in some cities than in others: for example, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"accff03e-b77c-4fcc-94ac-100faed8e173","content":"<p>there were 49 guilds in Bordeaux but 72 guilds in Lyon despite the two cities having nearly equal numbers of inhabitants.</p>"},{"id":"cc5b4ec6-66a9-4746-8dd7-24713b9c52a4","content":"<p>Lyon had far fewer inhabitants than Paris did but had many more guilds.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"589a5d90-c22e-42e0-b06a-1a5c7353b81b","content":"<p>there was one guild for every 661 inhabitants in Rouen but one guild for every 4,511 inhabitants in Paris.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"129e9ac6-fd46-4815-9c6e-8929564e447c","content":"<p>Paris had 133 guilds and 600,000 inhabitants, or one guild for every 4,511 inhabitants.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["589a5d90-c22e-42e0-b06a-1a5c7353b81b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The claim we&rsquo;re trying to prove is that guilds were much more numerous relative to population in some cities than others. This choice describes the guilds per number of inhabitants in two cities (Rouen and Paris), showing significant differences between guilds relative to population in these cities.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice misreads the table. Bordeaux had 80,000 inhabitants, according to the table, while Lyon had 143,000, so it isn&rsquo;t correct to say that they had "nearly equal numbers of inhabitants." Lyon had almost twice as many inhabitants. Choice B is incorrect. This choice misreads the table. Although Lyon did have fewer inhabitants than Paris, it didn&rsquo;t have more guilds&mdash;Paris had 133 guilds versus Lyon&rsquo;s 72. Choice D is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t support the claim. To show that guilds were more numerous relative to population in some cities than others, we would need to compare at least two cities, and this choice only mentions one.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-QUA-M005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$953f31af-a95a-4fd2-919b-1c53a90b980a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5ff1ba73","external_id":"7eb3995a-1328-4088-bfa1-661911b8e49b","vaultid":"1ea208a1-72cf-4322-b06f-98e062957d5b","uId":"d7ab0ec6-5387-477f-8809-6b0fbb75132b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Guilds in French Cities in the Late Eighteenth Century</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">City</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Date</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Inhabitants</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Number of guilds</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Inhabitants per guild</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Paris</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1766</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">600,000</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">133</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">4,511</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Bordeaux</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1762</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">80,000</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">49</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1,633</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Rouen</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1775</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">74,000</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">112</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">661</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Lyon</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1789</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">143,000</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">72</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1,986</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>Guilds&mdash;local associations of artisans and merchants in the same industry&mdash;were widespread in France from the medieval period until the late eighteenth century. But guilds were much more numerous relative to the population in some cities than in others: for example, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"accff03e-b77c-4fcc-94ac-100faed8e173","content":"<p>there were 49 guilds in Bordeaux but 72 guilds in Lyon despite the two cities having nearly equal numbers of inhabitants.</p>"},{"id":"cc5b4ec6-66a9-4746-8dd7-24713b9c52a4","content":"<p>Lyon had far fewer inhabitants than Paris did but had many more guilds.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"589a5d90-c22e-42e0-b06a-1a5c7353b81b","content":"<p>there was one guild for every 661 inhabitants in Rouen but one guild for every 4,511 inhabitants in Paris.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"129e9ac6-fd46-4815-9c6e-8929564e447c","content":"<p>Paris had 133 guilds and 600,000 inhabitants, or one guild for every 4,511 inhabitants.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["589a5d90-c22e-42e0-b06a-1a5c7353b81b"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The claim we&rsquo;re trying to prove is that guilds were much more numerous relative to population in some cities than others. This choice describes the guilds per number of inhabitants in two cities (Rouen and Paris), showing significant differences between guilds relative to population in these cities.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice misreads the table. Bordeaux had 80,000 inhabitants, according to the table, while Lyon had 143,000, so it isn&rsquo;t correct to say that they had \"nearly equal numbers of inhabitants.\" Lyon had almost twice as many inhabitants. Choice B is incorrect. This choice misreads the table. Although Lyon did have fewer inhabitants than Paris, it didn&rsquo;t have more guilds&mdash;Paris had 133 guilds versus Lyon&rsquo;s 72. Choice D is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t support the claim. To show that guilds were more numerous relative to population in some cities than others, we would need to compare at least two cities, and this choice only mentions one.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-QUA-M005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"953f31af-a95a-4fd2-919b-1c53a90b980a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959371,"updateDate":1691007959371}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959371, 1691007959371),
    ($SATQ$602b47c7$SATQ$, $SATQ$8867acef-9776-4099-b6bc-f5059e8a9574$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d95c68ea-c05c-4440-a81f-078c474f2eb8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Biologists have predicted that birds&rsquo; feather structures vary with habitat temperature, but this hadn&rsquo;t been tested in mountain environments. Ornithologist Sahas Barve studied feathers from 249 songbird species inhabiting different elevations&mdash;and thus experiencing different temperatures&mdash;in the Himalaya Mountains. He found that feathers of high-elevation species not only have a greater proportion of warming downy sections to flat and smooth sections than do feathers of low-elevation species, but high-elevation species&rsquo; feathers also tend to be longer, providing a thicker layer of insulation.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b2929b02-3deb-4efc-aab7-d82b5173b498","content":"<p>Barve&rsquo;s investigation shows that some species of Himalayan songbirds have evolved feathers that better regulate body temperature than do the feathers of other species, contradicting previous predictions.</p>"},{"id":"36344457-2a6e-46db-81c8-32faba49d2e4","content":"<p>Barve found an association between habitat temperature and feather structure among Himalayan songbirds, lending new support to a general prediction.</p>"},{"id":"18c279df-6531-48e1-8860-c4114c6b9d66","content":"<p>Barve discovered that songbirds have adapted to their environment by growing feathers without flat and smooth sections, complicating an earlier hypothesis.</p>"},{"id":"17cefe06-f214-4a2d-93f4-d110454b9b4a","content":"<p>The results of Barve&rsquo;s study suggest that the ability of birds to withstand cold temperatures is determined more strongly by feather length than feather structure, challenging an established belief.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["36344457-2a6e-46db-81c8-32faba49d2e4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The text describes how Barve found an association between habitat temperature and feather structure among Himalayan songbirds, which supports the general prediction that birds&rsquo; feather structures vary with habitat temperature. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Barve&rsquo;s study isn&rsquo;t said to contradict previous predictions. In fact, the study supports the prediction described in the first sentence, which is that birds&rsquo; feather structures vary with habitat temperature. Choice C is incorrect. Barve&rsquo;s study isn&rsquo;t said to &ldquo;complicate an earlier hypothesis.&rdquo; In fact, the study supports the earlier prediction described in the first sentence, which is that birds&rsquo; feather structures vary with habitat temperature. Choice D is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t compare the importance of feather length and feather structure, and it doesn&rsquo;t say that Barve&rsquo;s study challenges any established beliefs. In fact, the study supports the prediction described in the first sentence, which is that birds&rsquo; feather structures vary with habitat temperature. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CID-M009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c148052b-707e-4bc4-86b7-8496f472a353$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"602b47c7","external_id":"8867acef-9776-4099-b6bc-f5059e8a9574","vaultid":"d95c68ea-c05c-4440-a81f-078c474f2eb8","uId":"09e34a70-e3e9-4eb8-b76e-24c85ff6cfcf","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Biologists have predicted that birds&rsquo; feather structures vary with habitat temperature, but this hadn&rsquo;t been tested in mountain environments. Ornithologist Sahas Barve studied feathers from 249 songbird species inhabiting different elevations&mdash;and thus experiencing different temperatures&mdash;in the Himalaya Mountains. He found that feathers of high-elevation species not only have a greater proportion of warming downy sections to flat and smooth sections than do feathers of low-elevation species, but high-elevation species&rsquo; feathers also tend to be longer, providing a thicker layer of insulation.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b2929b02-3deb-4efc-aab7-d82b5173b498","content":"<p>Barve&rsquo;s investigation shows that some species of Himalayan songbirds have evolved feathers that better regulate body temperature than do the feathers of other species, contradicting previous predictions.</p>"},{"id":"36344457-2a6e-46db-81c8-32faba49d2e4","content":"<p>Barve found an association between habitat temperature and feather structure among Himalayan songbirds, lending new support to a general prediction.</p>"},{"id":"18c279df-6531-48e1-8860-c4114c6b9d66","content":"<p>Barve discovered that songbirds have adapted to their environment by growing feathers without flat and smooth sections, complicating an earlier hypothesis.</p>"},{"id":"17cefe06-f214-4a2d-93f4-d110454b9b4a","content":"<p>The results of Barve&rsquo;s study suggest that the ability of birds to withstand cold temperatures is determined more strongly by feather length than feather structure, challenging an established belief.</p>"}],"keys":["36344457-2a6e-46db-81c8-32faba49d2e4"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The text describes how Barve found an association between habitat temperature and feather structure among Himalayan songbirds, which supports the general prediction that birds&rsquo; feather structures vary with habitat temperature. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Barve&rsquo;s study isn&rsquo;t said to contradict previous predictions. In fact, the study supports the prediction described in the first sentence, which is that birds&rsquo; feather structures vary with habitat temperature. Choice C is incorrect. Barve&rsquo;s study isn&rsquo;t said to &ldquo;complicate an earlier hypothesis.&rdquo; In fact, the study supports the earlier prediction described in the first sentence, which is that birds&rsquo; feather structures vary with habitat temperature. Choice D is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t compare the importance of feather length and feather structure, and it doesn&rsquo;t say that Barve&rsquo;s study challenges any established beliefs. In fact, the study supports the prediction described in the first sentence, which is that birds&rsquo; feather structures vary with habitat temperature. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CID-M009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c148052b-707e-4bc4-86b7-8496f472a353","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959360,"updateDate":1691007959360}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959360, 1691007959360),
    ($SATQ$60c6b64d$SATQ$, $SATQ$c26900f9-e2f8-4720-8940-4d2f422a2ee6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Male t&uacute;ngara frogs make complex calls to attract mates, but their calls also attract frog-biting midges, insects that feed on the frogs&rsquo; blood. Researchers Ximena Bernal and Priyanka de Silva wondered if the calls alone are sufficient for midges to locate the frogs or if midges use carbon dioxide emitted by frogs as an additional cue to their prey&rsquo;s whereabouts, like mosquitoes do. In an experiment, the researchers placed two midge traps in a t&uacute;ngara frog breeding area. One trap played recordings of t&uacute;ngara frog calls and the other released carbon dioxide along with playing the calls. Bernal and de Silva concluded that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to frog-biting midges.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding from the experiment, if true, would most directly support Bernal and de Silva&rsquo;s conclusion?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e7bfc942-bb9a-483f-b127-8a0a46a1425f","content":"<p>Only a small number of midges were found in the traps, though the majority were found in the trap that played calls and released carbon dioxide.</p>"},{"id":"735ac17d-2d56-4aba-ae95-9839682cba22","content":"<p>Midges entered the trap that released carbon dioxide and played calls only during or immediately after periods of carbon dioxide release.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"0977459e-7cf4-4b3d-9e11-a22cb5e724f3","content":"<p>More midges were found in the trap that only played calls than in the trap that played calls and released carbon dioxide.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e8a435eb-48d6-4845-b404-4885ac5cc5c5","content":"<p>The trap that released carbon dioxide and played calls attracted few midges when carbon dioxide concentrations were low but attracted many midges when carbon dioxide concentrations were high.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0977459e-7cf4-4b3d-9e11-a22cb5e724f3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would most directly support the researchers&rsquo; conclusion that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to frog-biting midges regarding the location of male t&uacute;ngara frogs. The text begins by stating that the mating call of the male t&uacute;ngara frogs also attracts frog-biting midges, which prey on the frogs&rsquo; blood. Researchers Bernal and de Silva were curious if the mating calls were sufficient signals for the midges to locate the frogs or if midges also used carbon dioxide emitted by the frogs to locate their prey. The text then details the procedure the researchers used to investigate their question and summarizes their conclusion&mdash;that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to the midges. Thus, if more midges were found in the researchers&rsquo; trap that only played calls than in the trap that played calls and released carbon dioxide, it follows that the frog calls seem sufficient without the carbon dioxide cue. This finding supports the researchers&rsquo; conclusion that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to frog-biting midges.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because finding that the majority of frog-biting midges were found in the traps that both played the mating call and released carbon dioxide would present evidence that directly refutes the researchers&rsquo; conclusion&mdash;that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to the frog-biting midges regarding the location of t&uacute;ngara frogs. Choice B is incorrect because if the midges entered the trap that played calls and released carbon dioxide only during or immediately after carbon dioxide was released, that would suggest that the midges used the carbon dioxide as a way to locate their prey, a finding that would contradict the researchers&rsquo; conclusion that carbon dioxide was not an additional cue to the midges. Choice D is incorrect because a trap attracting larger numbers of midges with high carbon dioxide concentrations than a trap with low carbon dioxide concentrations suggests that carbon dioxide might serve as an additional cue to the midges as to the location on the frogs, a finding contrary to the researchers&rsquo; conclusion.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"60c6b64d","external_id":"c26900f9-e2f8-4720-8940-4d2f422a2ee6","vaultid":null,"uId":"0d402854-314c-4127-8917-7df649ff1f74","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Male t&uacute;ngara frogs make complex calls to attract mates, but their calls also attract frog-biting midges, insects that feed on the frogs&rsquo; blood. Researchers Ximena Bernal and Priyanka de Silva wondered if the calls alone are sufficient for midges to locate the frogs or if midges use carbon dioxide emitted by frogs as an additional cue to their prey&rsquo;s whereabouts, like mosquitoes do. In an experiment, the researchers placed two midge traps in a t&uacute;ngara frog breeding area. One trap played recordings of t&uacute;ngara frog calls and the other released carbon dioxide along with playing the calls. Bernal and de Silva concluded that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to frog-biting midges.</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding from the experiment, if true, would most directly support Bernal and de Silva&rsquo;s conclusion?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e7bfc942-bb9a-483f-b127-8a0a46a1425f","content":"<p>Only a small number of midges were found in the traps, though the majority were found in the trap that played calls and released carbon dioxide.</p>"},{"id":"735ac17d-2d56-4aba-ae95-9839682cba22","content":"<p>Midges entered the trap that released carbon dioxide and played calls only during or immediately after periods of carbon dioxide release.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"0977459e-7cf4-4b3d-9e11-a22cb5e724f3","content":"<p>More midges were found in the trap that only played calls than in the trap that played calls and released carbon dioxide.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e8a435eb-48d6-4845-b404-4885ac5cc5c5","content":"<p>The trap that released carbon dioxide and played calls attracted few midges when carbon dioxide concentrations were low but attracted many midges when carbon dioxide concentrations were high.</p>"}],"keys":["0977459e-7cf4-4b3d-9e11-a22cb5e724f3"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would most directly support the researchers&rsquo; conclusion that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to frog-biting midges regarding the location of male t&uacute;ngara frogs. The text begins by stating that the mating call of the male t&uacute;ngara frogs also attracts frog-biting midges, which prey on the frogs&rsquo; blood. Researchers Bernal and de Silva were curious if the mating calls were sufficient signals for the midges to locate the frogs or if midges also used carbon dioxide emitted by the frogs to locate their prey. The text then details the procedure the researchers used to investigate their question and summarizes their conclusion&mdash;that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to the midges. Thus, if more midges were found in the researchers&rsquo; trap that only played calls than in the trap that played calls and released carbon dioxide, it follows that the frog calls seem sufficient without the carbon dioxide cue. This finding supports the researchers&rsquo; conclusion that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to frog-biting midges.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because finding that the majority of frog-biting midges were found in the traps that both played the mating call and released carbon dioxide would present evidence that directly refutes the researchers&rsquo; conclusion&mdash;that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to the frog-biting midges regarding the location of t&uacute;ngara frogs. Choice B is incorrect because if the midges entered the trap that played calls and released carbon dioxide only during or immediately after carbon dioxide was released, that would suggest that the midges used the carbon dioxide as a way to locate their prey, a finding that would contradict the researchers&rsquo; conclusion that carbon dioxide was not an additional cue to the midges. Choice D is incorrect because a trap attracting larger numbers of midges with high carbon dioxide concentrations than a trap with low carbon dioxide concentrations suggests that carbon dioxide might serve as an additional cue to the midges as to the location on the frogs, a finding contrary to the researchers&rsquo; conclusion.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323044,"updateDate":1730147323044}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323044, 1730147323044),
    ($SATQ$61228830$SATQ$, $SATQ$cc7722c6-1049-4076-bffd-0613e48fd1d0$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$027e240f-4095-460e-8631-cc51b0486cae$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A heliograph is a semaphore device used for sending optical communications&mdash;usually in the form of Morse code&mdash;by reflecting flashes of sunlight off a mirror. Heliographs were used for rapid communication across expansive distances for military, surveying, and forestry purposes during the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, but they were largely effective only during the daytime, and the range of the device depended on factors such as the opacity of the air and line of sight. Therefore, heliographs were eventually replaced by technology that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f1b17566-50ab-4dfa-8162-0b9dfb83b845","content":"<p>worked on similar principles but was easier to produce and maintain.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"ce4c2b6f-ae38-4a37-a380-6b1c21671281","content":"<p>was not so constrained by environmental circumstances.</p>"},{"id":"e3d94608-7a32-4971-9020-9921737341e4","content":"<p>could be used for more than military, surveying, or forestry purposes.</p>"},{"id":"50304959-febe-4fac-b107-30742254efcf","content":"<p>enabled communication that didn&rsquo;t require knowledge of Morse code.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ce4c2b6f-ae38-4a37-a380-6b1c21671281"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The passage explains that heliographs &ldquo;were largely effective only during the daytime&rdquo; and that &ldquo;the range of the device depended on factors such as the opacity of the air and line of sight.&rdquo; These constraints would have greatly limited the use of the heliograph, so we can infer that this would have been a reason to replace it with new technology. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The passage doesn&rsquo;t discuss the production and maintenance of heliographs, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. Choice C is incorrect. The passage doesn&rsquo;t mention any &ldquo;other purposes&rdquo; that a heliograph wouldn&rsquo;t work for, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. Choice D is incorrect. The passage doesn&rsquo;t mention knowledge of Morse code as a particular problem with the use of heliographs, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-INF-H008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$defd19d9-0b41-47b0-8913-05be560922ec$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"61228830","external_id":"cc7722c6-1049-4076-bffd-0613e48fd1d0","vaultid":"027e240f-4095-460e-8631-cc51b0486cae","uId":"9c292bda-c1d8-4b43-8ae3-6df5ae4b0289","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A heliograph is a semaphore device used for sending optical communications&mdash;usually in the form of Morse code&mdash;by reflecting flashes of sunlight off a mirror. Heliographs were used for rapid communication across expansive distances for military, surveying, and forestry purposes during the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, but they were largely effective only during the daytime, and the range of the device depended on factors such as the opacity of the air and line of sight. Therefore, heliographs were eventually replaced by technology that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f1b17566-50ab-4dfa-8162-0b9dfb83b845","content":"<p>worked on similar principles but was easier to produce and maintain.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"ce4c2b6f-ae38-4a37-a380-6b1c21671281","content":"<p>was not so constrained by environmental circumstances.</p>"},{"id":"e3d94608-7a32-4971-9020-9921737341e4","content":"<p>could be used for more than military, surveying, or forestry purposes.</p>"},{"id":"50304959-febe-4fac-b107-30742254efcf","content":"<p>enabled communication that didn&rsquo;t require knowledge of Morse code.</p>"}],"keys":["ce4c2b6f-ae38-4a37-a380-6b1c21671281"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The passage explains that heliographs &ldquo;were largely effective only during the daytime&rdquo; and that &ldquo;the range of the device depended on factors such as the opacity of the air and line of sight.&rdquo; These constraints would have greatly limited the use of the heliograph, so we can infer that this would have been a reason to replace it with new technology. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The passage doesn&rsquo;t discuss the production and maintenance of heliographs, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. Choice C is incorrect. The passage doesn&rsquo;t mention any &ldquo;other purposes&rdquo; that a heliograph wouldn&rsquo;t work for, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. Choice D is incorrect. The passage doesn&rsquo;t mention knowledge of Morse code as a particular problem with the use of heliographs, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-INF-H008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"defd19d9-0b41-47b0-8913-05be560922ec","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959368,"updateDate":1691007959368}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959368, 1691007959368),
    ($SATQ$6177abc5$SATQ$, $SATQ$a9165713-4da8-48bf-91b0-7def09cee451$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Video Game Availability by Initial Release Years</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Initial release years</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Percentage of games still available</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">1975&ndash;1979</th><td style="text-align: center;">0.89</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">1980&ndash;1984</th><td style="text-align: center;">3.65</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">1985&ndash;1989</th><td style="text-align: center;">15.38</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">1990&ndash;1994</th><td style="text-align: center;">19.33</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">1995&ndash;1999</th><td style="text-align: center;">14.22</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>In a recent study, researchers found that relatively few video games released over the decades remain available today. For example, only 14.22 percent of games are still available that were initially released in <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0e456259-b320-42c5-9109-21a4ed8e1558","content":"<p>2000&ndash;2004.</p>"},{"id":"21003d8a-6c70-4128-b11c-5198ca655ba2","content":"<p>1995&ndash;1999.</p>"},{"id":"79717482-2fe9-46cc-8f14-c02e110b161e","content":"<p>1970&ndash;1974.</p>"},{"id":"54a4c064-c415-4583-9709-deaa9d1016a8","content":"<p>1985&ndash;1989.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["21003d8a-6c70-4128-b11c-5198ca655ba2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement about video game availability. The text states that just a few games released in the past are available today and then indicates that there is a period of years from which only 14.22 percent of the games released are available. The table shows that 14.22 percent of games are still available from the years 1995&ndash;1999.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the years 2000&ndash;2004 are not represented in the table. Choice C is incorrect because the years 1970&ndash;1974 are not represented in the table. Choice D is incorrect because the years 1985&ndash;1989 correspond to a percentage of games still available of 15.38 percent, not 14.22 percent.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6177abc5","external_id":"a9165713-4da8-48bf-91b0-7def09cee451","vaultid":null,"uId":"2012dd0e-7d61-4222-bd7e-fce114152e1e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Video Game Availability by Initial Release Years</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Initial release years</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Percentage of games still available</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">1975&ndash;1979</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.89</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">1980&ndash;1984</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">3.65</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">1985&ndash;1989</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">15.38</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">1990&ndash;1994</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">19.33</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">1995&ndash;1999</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">14.22</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>In a recent study, researchers found that relatively few video games released over the decades remain available today. For example, only 14.22 percent of games are still available that were initially released in <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0e456259-b320-42c5-9109-21a4ed8e1558","content":"<p>2000&ndash;2004.</p>"},{"id":"21003d8a-6c70-4128-b11c-5198ca655ba2","content":"<p>1995&ndash;1999.</p>"},{"id":"79717482-2fe9-46cc-8f14-c02e110b161e","content":"<p>1970&ndash;1974.</p>"},{"id":"54a4c064-c415-4583-9709-deaa9d1016a8","content":"<p>1985&ndash;1989.</p>"}],"keys":["21003d8a-6c70-4128-b11c-5198ca655ba2"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement about video game availability. The text states that just a few games released in the past are available today and then indicates that there is a period of years from which only 14.22 percent of the games released are available. The table shows that 14.22 percent of games are still available from the years 1995&ndash;1999.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the years 2000&ndash;2004 are not represented in the table. Choice C is incorrect because the years 1970&ndash;1974 are not represented in the table. Choice D is incorrect because the years 1985&ndash;1989 correspond to a percentage of games still available of 15.38 percent, not 14.22 percent.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669212,"updateDate":1743430669212}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669212, 1743430669212),
    ($SATQ$626a1308$SATQ$, $SATQ$691aa9c9-6158-43be-97a2-6230c41ccfe3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Data Sources for Neptune Temperature Analysis</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Instrument</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Observatory</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Data type</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Observation years</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">TEXES (Texas Echelon Cross Echelle Spectrograph)</th><td>Gemini Observatory</td><td>spectroscopy</td><td style="text-align: center;">2007, 2019</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">T-ReCS (Thermal-Region Camera Spectrograph)</th><td>Gemini Observatory</td><td>infrared imaging</td><td style="text-align: center;">2007, 2010</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">LWS (Long Wavelength Spectrometer)</th><td>Keck Observatory</td><td>infrared imaging</td><td style="text-align: center;">2003</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">VISIR (VLT Imager and Spectrometer for mid-InfraRed)</th><td>European Southern Observatory</td><td>spectroscopy</td><td style="text-align: center;">2006</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>Julianne I. Moses and colleagues have reported that Neptune may have cooled significantly between 2003 and 2020. The team reached this conclusion by analyzing existing infrared imaging and spectroscopy data about the planet obtained from various instruments in different years<em>.</em> Of the team&rsquo;s sources listed in the table, the earliest example of spectroscopy data included in the analysis was obtained in <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"22e1df7e-858a-4409-b6c3-375355fed997","content":"<p>2007 using TEXES at the Gemini Observatory.</p>"},{"id":"94bb20e3-aae4-4605-9872-8606811a0fa0","content":"<p>2007 using T-ReCS at the Gemini Observatory.</p>"},{"id":"75808ba0-9271-43e1-a845-ca21c0ecca86","content":"<p>2006 using VISIR at the European Southern Observatory.</p>"},{"id":"4219c614-889d-426e-9a31-5e0210cf8fa7","content":"<p>2003 using LWS at the W.M. Keck Observatory.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["75808ba0-9271-43e1-a845-ca21c0ecca86"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it effectively completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Moses and colleagues&rsquo; analysis of changes in Neptune&rsquo;s temperature by using data from the table to identify the source of the earliest example of spectroscopy data included in this analysis. The text indicates that Moses and colleagues analyzed infrared imaging and spectroscopy data about Neptune obtained in different years by various instruments. The table lists the observatories where these instruments are housed, the type of data each instrument collected (infrared imaging or spectroscopy), and the years in which the instruments&rsquo; observations were made. According to the table, only two of these instruments obtained spectroscopy data: VISIR at the European Southern Observatory, which made its observations in 2006, and TEXES at the Gemini Observatory, which made its observations in later years (2007 and 2019). Although two other instruments are also included in the table, they obtained infrared imaging data, not spectroscopy data. Therefore, the earliest example of spectroscopy data included in the researchers&rsquo; analysis is the data obtained by VISIR at the European Southern Observatory in 2006.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the table indicates that TEXES at the Gemini Observatory collected spectroscopy data in 2007 and 2019, the table also indicates that this type of data was collected in 2006 by VISIR at the European Southern Observatory. Because the VISIR data were collected in the previous year, the data obtained in 2007 by TEXES are not the earliest example of spectroscopy data included in Moses and team&rsquo;s analysis of Neptune&rsquo;s temperature. Choice B is incorrect because the table indicates that T-ReCS at the Gemini Observatory collected infrared imaging data, not spectroscopy data. T-ReCS therefore cannot be the source of the earliest example of spectroscopy data that is included in Moses and colleagues&rsquo; analysis of Neptune&rsquo;s temperature. Additionally, the T-ReCS data were collected in 2007, meaning that these data were obtained later than any type of data collected in 2006. Choice D is incorrect. Although the table indicates that data from LWS at the W.M. Keck Observatory were obtained in 2003, making these data the earliest example of any type of data included in the analysis, the table also indicates that LWS collected infrared imaging data, not spectroscopy data. LWS therefore cannot be the source of the earliest example of spectroscopy data included in Moses and team&rsquo;s analysis of Neptune&rsquo;s temperature.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"626a1308","external_id":"691aa9c9-6158-43be-97a2-6230c41ccfe3","vaultid":null,"uId":"6c95c834-0078-4724-b689-a7be45a43021","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Data Sources for Neptune Temperature Analysis</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Instrument</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Observatory</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Data type</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Observation years</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">TEXES (Texas Echelon Cross Echelle Spectrograph)</th><td>Gemini Observatory</td><td>spectroscopy</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2007, 2019</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">T-ReCS (Thermal-Region Camera Spectrograph)</th><td>Gemini Observatory</td><td>infrared imaging</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2007, 2010</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">LWS (Long Wavelength Spectrometer)</th><td>Keck Observatory</td><td>infrared imaging</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2003</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">VISIR (VLT Imager and Spectrometer for mid-InfraRed)</th><td>European Southern Observatory</td><td>spectroscopy</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2006</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>Julianne I. Moses and colleagues have reported that Neptune may have cooled significantly between 2003 and 2020. The team reached this conclusion by analyzing existing infrared imaging and spectroscopy data about the planet obtained from various instruments in different years<em>.</em> Of the team&rsquo;s sources listed in the table, the earliest example of spectroscopy data included in the analysis was obtained in <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"22e1df7e-858a-4409-b6c3-375355fed997","content":"<p>2007 using TEXES at the Gemini Observatory.</p>"},{"id":"94bb20e3-aae4-4605-9872-8606811a0fa0","content":"<p>2007 using T-ReCS at the Gemini Observatory.</p>"},{"id":"75808ba0-9271-43e1-a845-ca21c0ecca86","content":"<p>2006 using VISIR at the European Southern Observatory.</p>"},{"id":"4219c614-889d-426e-9a31-5e0210cf8fa7","content":"<p>2003 using LWS at the W.M. Keck Observatory.</p>"}],"keys":["75808ba0-9271-43e1-a845-ca21c0ecca86"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it effectively completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Moses and colleagues&rsquo; analysis of changes in Neptune&rsquo;s temperature by using data from the table to identify the source of the earliest example of spectroscopy data included in this analysis. The text indicates that Moses and colleagues analyzed infrared imaging and spectroscopy data about Neptune obtained in different years by various instruments. The table lists the observatories where these instruments are housed, the type of data each instrument collected (infrared imaging or spectroscopy), and the years in which the instruments&rsquo; observations were made. According to the table, only two of these instruments obtained spectroscopy data: VISIR at the European Southern Observatory, which made its observations in 2006, and TEXES at the Gemini Observatory, which made its observations in later years (2007 and 2019). Although two other instruments are also included in the table, they obtained infrared imaging data, not spectroscopy data. Therefore, the earliest example of spectroscopy data included in the researchers&rsquo; analysis is the data obtained by VISIR at the European Southern Observatory in 2006.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the table indicates that TEXES at the Gemini Observatory collected spectroscopy data in 2007 and 2019, the table also indicates that this type of data was collected in 2006 by VISIR at the European Southern Observatory. Because the VISIR data were collected in the previous year, the data obtained in 2007 by TEXES are not the earliest example of spectroscopy data included in Moses and team&rsquo;s analysis of Neptune&rsquo;s temperature. Choice B is incorrect because the table indicates that T-ReCS at the Gemini Observatory collected infrared imaging data, not spectroscopy data. T-ReCS therefore cannot be the source of the earliest example of spectroscopy data that is included in Moses and colleagues&rsquo; analysis of Neptune&rsquo;s temperature. Additionally, the T-ReCS data were collected in 2007, meaning that these data were obtained later than any type of data collected in 2006. Choice D is incorrect. Although the table indicates that data from LWS at the W.M. Keck Observatory were obtained in 2003, making these data the earliest example of any type of data included in the analysis, the table also indicates that LWS collected infrared imaging data, not spectroscopy data. LWS therefore cannot be the source of the earliest example of spectroscopy data included in Moses and team&rsquo;s analysis of Neptune&rsquo;s temperature.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061178,"updateDate":1755117061178}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061178, 1755117061178),
